(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-oxygen-delivery-vignettes"]={"title":"Oxygen Delivery Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 59-year-old man with pneumonia is started on a simple mask. Thirty minutes later he is breathing more easily, his lips are pink instead of dusky, and his saturation is up to his target. Which is the next step in evaluating his response?","choices":["Keep checking vitals and watch for complications","Stop oxygen now that he looks better","Increase to a non-rebreather mask","Order a home oxygen qualification today"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. He looks better and his saturation reached his target, which covers symptom improvement, color and the objective saturation. Evaluating response also includes watching vital signs and monitoring further for complications, so ongoing checks are the next step, not a stopping point. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man is placed on a non-rebreather mask at 10 L/min for severe hypoxia. The nurse notices the reservoir bag collapses completely each time he breathes in. Which adjustment is needed?","choices":["Increase the flow until the bag stays inflated","Switch to a nasal cannula at 6 L/min","Remove the one-way valve","Lower the flow to 5 L/min"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A bag that collapses completely on every breath means the flow is too low for his inspiratory demand, and a collapsed reservoir cannot deliver the high fraction. A non-rebreather needs 10-15 L/min, and the bag must not collapse on inspiration, so raise the flow until it stays inflated. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has poor finger perfusion, and the pulse oximeter will not give a steady reading. His arterial oxygen pressure is 55 mm Hg on room air at rest. What is his home oxygen status?","choices":["Qualifies in Group I","Qualifies in Group II","Cannot qualify without oximetry","Does not qualify at 55 mm Hg"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Documentation can use an arterial blood gas or pulse oximetry. An arterial oxygen pressure of 55 mm Hg or less qualifies at Group I, and his is 55, so he qualifies without the oximeter. Group I is recertified at 12 months. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is admitted with a flare. His oxygen saturation is 86% on room air, and the team wants to give him an exact, adjustable fraction of inspired oxygen rather than a fraction that changes with his breathing. Which delivery device is the best choice?","choices":["Nasal cannula","Simple mask","Venturi mask","Non-rebreather mask"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. He has chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and the team wants a precise, adjustable fraction of inspired oxygen that does not change with his breathing. The Venturi mask is an air-entrainment device: different ports set the fraction (24-50%), and it stays fixed whatever his demand. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 77-year-old woman is going home on oxygen tanks. Her son asks what to do with the tanks and how to store them. Which property of the tanks drives the storage and disposal teaching?","choices":["They hold compressed gas","They hold a liquid fuel","They emit carbon monoxide","They spoil within a week"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen tanks contain compressed gas, and that property drives the teaching on proper storage and disposal. Her son should store the tanks as the teaching directs and not discard them as ordinary trash. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man admitted with a heart failure exacerbation still needs 2 L/min of oxygen on hospital day 1. His team plans to discharge him in a few days. When should his home oxygen qualification be performed?","choices":["Once he no longer needs oxygen","After a year on home oxygen","At his first outpatient visit","At the time of need, before discharge"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The qualification is documented at the time of need, and he still needs 2 L/min with discharge planned in a few days. So the qualification is performed while he is in the hospital, before he leaves, so that home oxygen can be arranged. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with bronchitis is on a nasal cannula at 6 L/min with a saturation of 89%. He now has mild to moderate respiratory distress but is ventilating well. Which step fits his severity?","choices":["Turn the cannula to 10 L/min","Go straight to a bag-valve mask","Stop oxygen and recheck in an hour","Change to a simple mask at 5 to 10 L/min"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. He has mild to moderate respiratory distress, and his saturation of 89% persists on a cannula at 6 L/min, near the cannula's limit of 44%. A simple mask, a low flow device set at 5-10 L/min, is the step for this severity. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman with chronic lung disease and a 60-year-old man with pneumonia are both on oxygen. The nurse asks what saturation target to use for each. Which principle applies?","choices":["Both should be kept at 88%","Both should match room air","The target varies with the patient","Both should be kept at 100%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Response to oxygen is judged by saturation that improves to a target that varies with the patient. Her chronic lung disease and his pneumonia are different situations, so one number does not fit both, and each target is set for the patient. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man on home oxygen at 5 L/min by nasal cannula with no humidifier complains of a sore, dry nose and occasional bleeding. Which change addresses the cause?","choices":["Apply a petroleum lip and nose balm","Switch to a non-rebreather mask","Add humidity to the oxygen","Increase the flow to 6 L/min"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. He is on 5 L/min by nasal cannula with no humidifier, and has a sore, dry nose with bleeding. That is mucosal irritation. Humidity is used with cannula flow rates greater than 4 L/min, so adding humidity to the oxygen treats the cause. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man starting home oxygen asks why he must be so careful at home if oxygen is \"just air.\" Which property of oxygen underlies the safety teaching?","choices":["It is heavier than water","It turns into carbon monoxide","It is highly flammable","It is toxic to breathe for visitors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen is highly flammable, which is why home oxygen teaching includes no smoking in the home, staying away from heat sources, avoiding petroleum products and posting signs. Oxygen is not just air, and fire is a listed hazard. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with pneumonia is on a nasal cannula at 2 L/min. Her flow is increased to 5 L/min. About what fraction of inspired oxygen is she receiving now?","choices":["28%","40%","44%","60%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A nasal cannula at 2 L/min gives about 28%, and each added liter raises the fraction by about 4%. Going from 2 to 5 L/min adds 3 liters, which is 3 x 4% = 12%, so 28% + 12% = 40%. This matches the listed value of 40% at 5 L/min. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with a severe exacerbation of his chronic lung disease is too short of breath to use his inhaler properly. Which delivery method is preferred for his inhaled medication?","choices":["An oropharyngeal airway","Nebulizer","His usual inhaler, used faster","A trach collar"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He is in a severe exacerbation of chronic lung disease and too short of breath to use his inhaler properly. Inhalers are challenging in this situation, and a nebulizer delivers liquid medication directly to the airways without needing his coordination. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with heart failure is started on oxygen. An hour later her arterial oxygen pressure has risen, but her arterial carbon dioxide has also risen and her pH has fallen. How should this response be read?","choices":["A good response: pH is moving","Cannot be judged without a chest film","Not a good response: carbon dioxide is rising","A good response: oxygen pressure has risen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A good response needs the arterial oxygen pressure to rise without a rise in arterial carbon dioxide, along with correction of the pH. Hers rose, but so did her carbon dioxide while the pH fell, so the oxygen is not working as it should and she needs close reassessment. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man starting home oxygen says he smokes on his back porch and that his wife smokes in the kitchen. Which teaching applies?","choices":["His wife may smoke if he is in another room","Smoking is fine if the tank is turned off","He must stop; visitors are exempt","No one should smoke in the home"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Home oxygen teaching is that oxygen is highly flammable, and no one should smoke in the home. He smokes on the porch and his wife smokes in the kitchen. The rule covers the whole home, so both must follow it, and the fire hazard applies to everyone. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman using home oxygen keeps an electric space heater beside her recliner, where she sits with her tubing. Which teaching applies?","choices":["Lower her flow while the heater runs","Keep the heater 3 feet away","Keep oxygen at least 10 feet from the heater","A heater is safe because it has no flame"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Home oxygen teaching is to keep oxygen at least 10 feet from heat sources, including electric appliances as well as pilot lights and candles. A space heater beside her recliner is a heat source, so she must keep her oxygen at least 10 feet away. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has mild hypoxia, saturation 87%, during a cold. A student suggests a non-rebreather mask at 15 L/min \"to be safe.\" Why should that be avoided?","choices":["It cannot raise his saturation","It causes vasoconstriction in the lungs","It is a fire hazard in hospitals","It risks carbon dioxide retention"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. He has chronic obstructive pulmonary disease with only mild hypoxia (87%), and a non-rebreather at 15 L/min delivers a very high fraction (60-100%). Carbon dioxide retention is a listed hazard of oxygen, so a high-fraction device for a mild problem risks it. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman going home on oxygen asks what signs to post. Which signage is taught?","choices":["A sign with her recertification date","No Smoking and Oxygen in Use signs","A sign with her oxygen flow rate","A sign with her pharmacy's number"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Home oxygen teaching is to post No Smoking signage, and potentially Oxygen in Use signs, within the home. Oxygen is highly flammable, and the signs warn everyone who enters, including visitors and firefighters. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy with wheezing cannot coordinate pressing a metered-dose inhaler with breathing in, even with coaching. Which delivery method is the rationale for his medication?","choices":["A larger-dose inhaler","A non-rebreather mask","A Venturi mask","A nebulizer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A 4-year-old with wheezing cannot coordinate pressing a metered-dose inhaler with breathing in, even with coaching. Inhalers are challenging in certain situations, and a nebulizer delivers liquid medication directly to the airways without that coordination. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with bronchitis is placed on a simple mask at 6 to 7 L/min. About what fraction of inspired oxygen is he receiving?","choices":["28%","40%","50%","80%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A simple mask is a low flow device set at 5-10 L/min, and its fraction depends on the flow: 5-6 L/min gives 35%, 6-7 L/min gives 40%, and 7-8 L/min gives 50%. At 6 to 7 L/min he is receiving about 40%. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman develops severe hypoxia, saturation 79%, from pneumonia. She is alert, breathing at a steady rate with good chest rise, and her arterial carbon dioxide is normal. Which device delivers the high fraction she needs?","choices":["Non-rebreather mask","Nasal cannula","Simple mask","Bag-valve mask"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her saturation of 79% is severe hypoxia, yet she is alert, breathing steadily with good chest rise, so she is still ventilating well. The non-rebreather mask is the device for severely hypoxic patients who are still ventilating well, giving 60-100% at 10-15 L/min through its reservoir bag. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with chronic lung disease is assessed for home oxygen before discharge. Her resting room-air saturation is exactly 88%, and she has no other findings. What is her qualification?","choices":["Group I, recertified at 12 months","Group II, recertified at 3 months","Not qualified without a comorbidity","Qualified for exertion oxygen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Group I qualifies at a resting room-air saturation of 88% or less, and hers is exactly 88%, which meets the cutoff. Group I patients are recertified at 12 months, and no comorbidity is needed. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is ready for discharge after his treatment is optimized. His resting saturation on room air is 87%, and he has no pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale (right-sided heart failure from lung disease) or high hematocrit. How often will his home oxygen be recertified?","choices":["Every 1 month","Every 3 months","Every 12 months","Every 24 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His resting room-air saturation is 87%, at or below 88%, which is the Group I qualification (conditional Group II needs a comorbidity such as pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale or a hematocrit above 56%). Group I patients are recertified every 12 months. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with asthma is started on oxygen. She says she feels better, but her lips remain dusky, her accessory muscles are still working hard, and her saturation has not moved. How should her response be judged?","choices":["Not yet adequate on clinical and objective measures","Adequate, because she feels better","Adequate, because oxygen is running","Cannot be judged without a chest film"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. She feels better, but her lips are still dusky, her accessory muscles still work hard, and her saturation has not moved. Response is judged by symptoms, work of breathing, color and perfusion plus objective data. Most of those have not improved, so it is not yet adequate. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with chest pain has a myocardial infarction with ST elevation. He is alert, with an oxygen saturation of 96% on room air. Which is the right approach to oxygen?","choices":["Give a nasal cannula at 2 L/min","Give a Venturi mask at 28%","Withhold oxygen at this saturation","Give a non-rebreather at 15 L/min"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. He is alert with a saturation of 96% on room air, well above the 91% threshold, and oxygen is not given in a myocardial infarction unless the saturation is below 91%, because oxygen causes vasoconstriction. With no hypoxia, no oxygen is the right plan. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with a chronic lung disease flare needs both inhaled medication by nebulizer and supplemental oxygen. He has no tracheostomy. Which air-entrainment interface over his face suits him?","choices":["Nasal cannula","Aerosol mask","Trach collar","Non-rebreather mask"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He needs a nebulizer for his inhaled medication along with oxygen, and he has no tracheostomy. The aerosol mask is an air-entrainment nebulizer interface that fits over the face, so it delivers oxygen and nebulized medication together. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with pneumonia has an arterial blood gas before and after starting oxygen. After oxygen, his arterial oxygen pressure is higher, his carbon dioxide is unchanged, and his pH has returned toward normal. How is this response read?","choices":["A good response","Carbon dioxide retention","No response","A response if saturation reaches 100%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A good response is an increase in arterial oxygen pressure without an increase in arterial carbon dioxide, plus correction of the pH. His oxygen pressure rose, his carbon dioxide stayed unchanged, and his pH moved back toward normal, which meets all three. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with diabetes and an acute myocardial infarction has an oxygen saturation of 87% on room air. Which is the right approach to oxygen?","choices":["Withhold oxygen because of vasoconstriction","Wait for an arterial blood gas first","Withhold oxygen because of her diabetes","Give oxygen, since saturation is below 91%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her saturation of 87% is below the 91% cutoff. Oxygen is held back in a myocardial infarction because of vasoconstriction, but only while the saturation is 91% or higher. She is hypoxic, so the benefit of correcting hypoxia wins and she should receive oxygen. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with influenza has mild to moderate respiratory distress and a saturation of 88%. She keeps mouth-breathing because her nose is congested. Which low flow device delivers through both nose and mouth?","choices":["Nasal cannula","Non-rebreather mask","Trach collar","Simple mask"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. She has mild to moderate respiratory distress with a saturation of 88%, and with a congested nose she breathes through her mouth. A simple mask is a low flow device for mild to moderate distress that covers nose and mouth, so oxygen reaches her whichever way she breathes. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a daytime saturation of 93%. Overnight oximetry shows his saturation falling to 87% for long stretches. What does he qualify for?","choices":["Oxygen during exertion","Nocturnal oxygen","Continuous oxygen at rest","Nothing, since daytime is normal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His daytime saturation of 93% is normal, but overnight it falls to 87%. The sleep criterion is a saturation of 88% or less with a drop of 5% or more from baseline: 87% is under 88%, and 93 minus 87 is a 6% drop. He qualifies for nocturnal oxygen. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with pneumonia is on a Venturi mask. She is breathing fast and deep, and her saturation dips each time she takes a big breath. Which dosing principle is being violated?","choices":["The fraction must stay under 0.50","Total flow must exceed inspiratory demand","The bag must not collapse on inspiration","Humidity is needed above 4 L/min"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The Venturi mask is an air-entrainment device that delivers a fixed fraction only if the total flow stays higher than her inspiratory demand. She is breathing fast and deep and her saturation dips with big breaths, which suggests her demand exceeds the flow. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 17"}]};
